body:
Ok, testing failed so I'm reviewing the code. This part of insertAt() looks fishy:

```
    } else if (available == 0) {
        // Open addressing requires that we have at least 1 slot available. Need to refresh
        // the arrays to clear any removed elements.
        rehash(capacity());
    }
```

Shouldn't that be rehash(capacity() + 1) at least? This will rehash the hashmap to the same size it already has, violating the rule of keeping at least one slot available. And if you violate that, the next call to put() won't find any AVAILABLE/REMOVED slot, and because the capacity check/rehash is only performed by insertAt(), put() doesn't call that unless there's some available slot, so this will produce exactly the assertion I saw.

@opinali sounds fishy for sure.  Shouldn't we also double it and not just use +1 ?

Something like:

``` java
rehash(capacity() <<1);
```

@nmittler @opinali wdyt ?

Yes I see no reason to not double (or use whatever same growing factor used by the size>maxSize case). The code could be simplified to:

```
    if (size > maxSize || available == 0) {
        rehash(capacity() * 2);
    }
```

@opinali I'm starting to think the original code was correct.  maxSize is the size at which we have to grow (defaults to 0.5 \* capacity). If we add a bunch of entries and then delete them, the number of AVAILABLE slots could eventually become 0 since they have been marked as REMOVED. In this case, we rehash with the current capacity to clear out the REMOVED nodes and make them AVAILABLE again.  We shouldn't need to grow in this case.

I suspect that the problem you're running into is related to the choice of capacity.  This choice is important for reducing collisions in open addressing and to this point I've just been ensuring that it's odd.  Using prime numbers for capacity, however, is the typical approach and I think we should migrate to this before making any other changes.

Sound reasonable?
